 3. Trump sticks by Lutnick despite Nvidia inaction 
 
 By Maria Curi 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 President Trump and Commerce Secretary Howard Lutnick at an Oct. 2026 campaign event. Photo: Nathaniel Wilder/Bloomberg via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 The White House is standing by Commerce Secretary Howard Lutnick despite a view within the administration that Lutnick delayed action to address concerns over chip smuggling to China. 

 Why it matters: Congress smells blood in the water following bombshell reports that U.S. national security may have been jeopardized by Huang's relationship with a suspected Chinese spy. 

 Lawmakers want to know what the Trump administration did or did not do in response to that intelligence — and Lutnick, the official in charge of the department at the center of export control policy, is squarely in their field of view. Trump, however, is not distancing himself from the secretary. 

 "Secretary Lutnick is a champion of President Trump's America First trade and tariff policies, which have secured almost 20 fair trade deals and trillions in manufacturing investments," a White House official tells Axios. Catch up quick: Lutnick was sent intelligence about Huang's relationship with an alleged Chinese spy and repeatedly did not take export control action — despite pressure from the broader administration and the president himself, Axios first reported.

 What they're saying: Lutnick's role is prompting scrutiny on the Hill from members on both sides of the aisle. 

 "Secretary Howard Lutnick and Under Secretary Jeffrey Kessler must explain why they have not closed export control loopholes and act today to ensure our advanced technology is not fueling China's military," Sen. Elizabeth Warren (D-Mass.), poised to be the next Senate Banking Committee chair, said in a statement. "The obvious question is why did he choose to sell advanced chips?" Sen. John Kennedy (R-La.) told Politico. "Secretary Lutnick needs to answer that in front of God and country," Kennedy added. "I mean, there will be a congressional investigation. But I don't think he ought to wait on Congress." The Commerce Department has not confirmed for the record whether or not Lutnick has seen the memo.

 Yes, but: " It's impossible that Howard Lutnick didn't know about this memo," one senior administration official told Axios.

 "Of course Commerce got the memo. It had to get the memo. That's the point of the memo. It's about chips. Commerce deals with chips," a second official said. Go deeper. 

 
 
